<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Community-acquired pneumonia, right lower lobe (J18.9)</content>. Clinically resolved: afebrile since the third day, normal oxygen saturation and a clear chest; a mild cough and tiredness can last several weeks. Because he improved within a week and has never smoked, a follow-up chest radiograph is not indicated. First episode, so no recurrent-pneumonia evaluation. [S]<list><item>Gradual return to full days over two weeks.</item><item>Return for fever, breathlessness or a cough lasting beyond eight weeks.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Unintended weight loss with nutritional risk (R63.4)</content>. Weight fell 1.3 kg during the pneumonia. [S]<list><item>Supplement twice a day until weight returns to 94 kg.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Complete edentulism with two healing implants (K08.103)</content>. Implants healing well nine weeks after surgery. [S]<list><item>Attachment connection in four weeks as planned.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Post-pneumonia clinical resolution: symptoms, temperature, oxygen saturation and chest examination</td><td>One to two weeks after diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Afebrile, oxygen saturation 97%, chest clear; mild cough</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Follow-up chest radiograph</td><td>At six to eight weeks only for persistent symptoms, smokers eligible for lung cancer screening, or recurrent same-lobe pneumonia</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Improved within a week; never smoked; first episode</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Antibiotic course and adverse effects</td><td>At completion</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Five days of amoxicillin completed; no rash or diarrhea</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Vaccination status after pneumonia (pneumococcal, influenza, COVID-19, RSV)</td><td>At follow-up</td><td>Up to date</td><td>PCV20 ten weeks ago; influenza vaccine in the autumn; COVID-19 to be decided in the autumn; RSV not indicated at 58 without risk conditions</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Recurrent-pneumonia evaluation (immunoglobulins, HIV test, chest CT, swallowing)</td><td>After two or more episodes in a year or three in a lifetime</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>First episode; HIV test nonreactive at the preventive visit</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Oral mucosal examination and oral cancer screening (lips, cheeks, tongue, floor of mouth, palate, ridges, neck nodes)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Normal</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Implant sites and peri-implant mucosa</td><td>One to two weeks after surgery, at attachment connection, then at each review and yearly</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Healthy mucosa over both implants nine weeks after surgery</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Weight</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>92.9 kg, down 1.3 kg during the pneumonia</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Nutrition screen (MNA-SF, recorded as narrative)</td><td>At baseline when at risk, after about three months, and at six months</td><td>Up to date</td><td>12 of 14 at twelve weeks; repeat at six months</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Denture hygiene: daily cleaning, dentures out overnight, ridge and tongue cleaning</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Daily cleaning continued through the illness</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Oral ulcers healed within two weeks of relieving the denture, without need for a biopsy. Outcome by 2026-02-02: Both ulcers healed at two weeks; no biopsy needed [S]</item><item>New complete dentures that are comfortable and stable for eating and speaking. Outcome by 2026-04-13: Delivered six weeks before the twelve-week review; one adjustment; no sore spots now [S]</item><item>A two-implant lower overdenture in function. In progress; target 2026-07-13 [S]</item><item>Regain weight and a normal nutrition screen. In progress; target 2026-07-13 [S]</item><item>Speak in public without the lower denture moving. In progress; target 2026-07-13 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: The McGill consensus statement on overdentures. Mandibular two-implant overdentures as first choice standard of care for edentulous patients (2002); Evidence-based clinical practice guideline for the evaluation of potentially malignant disorders in the oral cavity: A report of the American Dental Association (2017); Validation of the Mini Nutritional Assessment short-form (MNA-SF): a practical tool for identification of nutritional status (2009); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, pneumonia monograph (2026); Expanded Recommendations for Use of Pneumococcal Conjugate Vaccines Among Adults Aged ≥50 Years: Recommendations of the Advisory Committee on Immunization Practices — United States, 2024 (2024); Diagnosis and Treatment of Adults with Community-acquired Pneumonia. An Official Clinical Practice Guideline of the American Thoracic Society and Infectious Diseases Society of America (2019)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Attachment connection with prosthodontics in four weeks. Six-month review in seven weeks. Next scheduled visit 2026-07-13: six-month review: the implant-retained lower overdenture in use for three weeks; chewing, speech, nutrition and long-term maintenance. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
